+## Highest-priority release actions
+
+Before release, the following should be treated as blocking controls:
+
+1. Wire fraud screening into both immediate and queued payment paths, fail closed, persist the decision, and prevent unscreened dispatch (F-01/F-02).
+2. Make scheme and ledger uncertainty durable and recoverable. Reuse `P{id}` and the persisted `ledgerReference`; add reconciliation/status queries before any retry (F-03/F-04/F-05/F-10).
+3. Prevent concurrent overspend and duplicate requests with account-level concurrency control and idempotency (F-04/F-07).
+4. Add bank-holiday handling and durable, observable queue processing with overdue-payment alerts (F-08/F-09).
+5. Implement closure approval only after an authoritative ledger zero-balance check and in-flight-payment check (F-16).
+6. Establish production-grade persistence, availability, monitoring, structured audit, and reconciliation controls (F-14/F-18/F-19/F-20).
+
+## Detection and operating metrics
+
+At minimum, operations should receive alerts or daily exception records for:
+
+- payments without a persisted fraud decision, payments stuck in `RECEIVED`, `SUBMITTED`, `POSTING_FAILED`, `QUEUED`, or an uncertainty state, and queue items past their execution date;
+- scheme instructions with no local payment, local sent payments with no scheme settlement, and ledger postings without the expected scheme reference;
+- duplicate idempotency keys, duplicate scheme/ledger references, balance-versus-ledger differences, and per-account limit violations;
+- fraud-screening timeouts/errors, scheme/ledger timeout rates, notification failures, 5xx/401/403 spikes, database/queue lag, clock skew, and statement freshness/completeness;
+- closure approvals without a successful ledger zero-balance result and all payment/audit records required for the decision.
